  <Fragment slot="content">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-black tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white">What Is a Cylinder?</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          A <strong>cylinder</strong> is one of the most essential three-dimensional geometric solids. It is defined by two congruent, parallel circular flat faces (called <strong>bases</strong>) interconnected by a smooth, curved lateral surface. Because the cross-section parallel to the bases remains identical everywhere along its height, a cylinder is mathematically classified as a curvilinear prism.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the central axis connecting the center points of both circular bases is strictly perpendicular (orthogonal at a 90° angle) to the base planes, the solid is termed a <strong>right circular cylinder</strong>. Right circular cylinders are the universal foundation for real-world storage tanks, fluid pipelines, hydraulic pistons, canned goods, and structural columns.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Educational Diagram 1: Right Circular Cylinder -->
    <div class="my-6 text-center">
      <img
        src="/images/math/right-circular-cylinder-volume.svg"
        alt="Right circular cylinder diagram illustrating circular top and bottom bases, radius r, diameter d, perpendicular height h, and core volume formula V = pi * r^2 * h"
        class="mx-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs max-w-full sm:max-w-lg"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 font-medium">
        Figure 1: Anatomy of a standard right circular cylinder with radius <em>r</em>, diameter <em>d = 2r</em>, and orthogonal perpendicular height <em>h</em>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">The Cylinder Volume Formula: V = πr²h</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          To understand why the cylinder volume formula is so straightforward, recall the universal rule for any right prism-like solid:
        </p>
        <div class="my-3 rounded-2xl border border-blue-200 bg-blue-50/50 p-4 font-mono text-center text-base sm:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:border-blue-900/60 dark:bg-blue-950/40 dark:text-blue-300">
          Volume = Base Area × Perpendicular Height
        </div>
        <p>
          The base of a circular cylinder is a flat circle of radius <em>r</em>. The area of a circle is calculated by the famous formula <code>Area = π × r²</code>. When you extrude this circular area uniformly along a perpendicular height <em>h</em>, you sweep out the total 3D capacity:
        </p>
        <div class="my-3 rounded-2xl border border-blue-200 bg-blue-50/50 p-4 font-mono text-center text-base sm:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:border-blue-900/60 dark:bg-blue-950/40 dark:text-blue-300">
          V = π × r² × h
        </div>
        <p>
          Where:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 list-disc list-inside">
          <li><strong>V</strong> = Three-dimensional volume (measured in cubic units like cm³, m³, or in³)</li>
          <li><strong>π (Pi)</strong> = The mathematical constant approximately equal to 3.14159265...</li>
          <li><strong>r</strong> = The radius of the circular base (half of the diameter)</li>
          <li><strong>h</strong> = The perpendicular height separating the two circular bases</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Calculating Cylinder Volume from Diameter</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          In engineering, architecture, and plumbing, it is often much easier to measure the total outer or inner <strong>diameter (d)</strong> of a pipe or tank with calipers rather than locating the exact center point to find the radius.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because diameter is twice the radius (<code>d = 2r</code>), the radius is simply half the diameter: <code>r = d / 2</code>. Substituting <code>(d / 2)</code> into the primary volume equation:
        </p>
        <div class="my-2 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/80 p-3 font-mono text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          V = π × (d / 2)² × h = π × (d² / 4) × h = (π × d² × h) / 4
        </div>
        <p>
          This diameter formula produces identical mathematical precision without having to divide by 2 as a separate preliminary step.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Hollow Cylinder / Cylindrical Shell Volume</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          A <strong>hollow cylinder</strong> (also referred to as a <em>cylindrical shell</em>, tube, pipe, straw, or sleeve) consists of a solid cylinder with a concentric cylindrical core bored straight through its center. When engineers need to determine the material volume of steel in a pipeline, concrete in a circular culvert, or aluminum in a beverage can, they calculate the volume of this cylindrical shell.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Educational Diagram 2: Hollow Cylinder -->
    <div class="my-6 text-center">
      <img
        src="/images/math/hollow-cylinder-volume.svg"
        alt="Hollow cylinder diagram showing outer radius R, inner radius r, wall thickness t, height h, and shell volume formula V = pi * (R^2 - r^2) * h"
        class="mx-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs max-w-full sm:max-w-lg"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 font-medium">
        Figure 2: Geometric anatomy of a hollow cylindrical shell showing outer radius <em>R</em>, inner radius <em>r</em>, wall thickness <em>t = R − r</em>, and perpendicular height <em>h</em>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Derivation of the Hollow Cylinder Formula</h3>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-2 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The volume of material contained in the shell is simply the difference between the total outer cylinder volume and the hollow inner core volume:
        </p>
        <div class="my-2 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/80 p-3 font-mono text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          V_shell = V_outer − V_inner = (π × R² × h) − (π × r² × h) = π × (R² − r²) × h
        </div>
        <p>
          Expressed using outer diameter <em>D</em> and inner diameter <em>d</em>:
        </p>
        <div class="my-2 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/80 p-3 font-mono text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          V_shell = [π × (D² − d²) × h] / 4
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
          <strong>Mandatory Geometric Constraint:</strong> The outer radius must strictly exceed the inner radius (<code>R &gt; r &gt; 0</code>). The wall thickness is <code>t = R − r = (D − d) / 2</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Oblique Cylinders &amp; Cavalieri's Principle</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          An <strong>oblique cylinder</strong> is a cylinder where the central axis is tilted at an acute angle rather than standing perpendicular to the circular bases. The two circular bases remain strictly parallel to one another, but the lateral surface slopes sideways.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Educational Diagram 3: Oblique Cylinder -->
    <div class="my-6 text-center">
      <img
        src="/images/math/oblique-cylinder-volume.svg"
        alt="Oblique cylinder diagram illustrating perpendicular height h versus slanted side length L and slant inclination angle theta"
        class="mx-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs max-w-full sm:max-w-lg"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 font-medium">
        Figure 3: Oblique cylinder demonstrating Cavalieri’s principle: volume depends on orthogonal perpendicular height <em>h</em>, not the longer slanted side length <em>L</em>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">The Golden Rule: Perpendicular Height vs. Slant Length</h3>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-2 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          According to <strong>Cavalieri's Principle</strong>, if two solids have equal heights and identical cross-sectional areas at every plane parallel to their bases, they have equal volumes. Imagine a neat vertical stack of circular coins: if you push the stack so that it leans sideways into an oblique slant, the amount of metal in the stack does not change.
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore, the volume of an oblique cylinder is still:
        </p>
        <div class="my-2 rounded-xl border border-purple-200 bg-purple-50/50 p-3 font-mono text-base font-bold text-purple-800 dark:border-purple-900/60 dark:bg-purple-950/40 dark:text-purple-300 text-center">
          V = π × r² × h
        </div>
        <p>
          <strong>Crucial Warning:</strong> <em>h</em> is the vertical perpendicular distance between the parallel base planes. Never use the slanted edge length <em>L</em> directly as height! If you only know the slant side length <em>L</em> and its inclination angle <em>θ</em> relative to the horizontal base plane, resolve the perpendicular height trigonometrically:
        </p>
        <div class="my-2 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/80 p-3 font-mono text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 text-center">
          h = L × sin(θ) &nbsp;⟹&nbsp; V = π × r² × [L × sin(θ)]
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Reverse Calculations: Finding Missing Dimensions</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          In manufacturing and design, you often start with a target volume (such as designing a 500 mL beverage container or a 10,000-liter storage tank) and need to solve for the necessary height or radius.
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-2">
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Solve for Height (Given V and r):</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 font-mono">
              V = π × r² × h ⟹ h = V / (π × r²)
            </p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Divide the volume by the circular base area.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Solve for Radius (Given V and h):</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 font-mono">
              V = π × r² × h ⟹ r = √(V / (π × h))
            </p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Divide volume by (πh) and take the square root. Diameter is then <code>d = 2r</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Step-by-Step Worked Examples (Article-Only)</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        
        <!-- Example 1 -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 shadow-2xs dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white text-sm">
            Example 1: Standard Right Cylinder (Radius + Height)
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-1">
            <strong>Problem:</strong> A cylindrical hot water tank has a radius of 30 cm and a height of 120 cm. Find its volume in cubic centimeters and liters.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-2 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-3 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-950/60 space-y-1">
            <p>1. Base Area = π × r² = π × (30)² = 900π ≈ 2,827.43 cm²</p>
            <p>2. Volume = Base Area × h = 2,827.43 × 120 ≈ 339,292 cm³</p>
            <p>3. Convert to Liters: 1 L = 1,000 cm³ ⟹ 339,292 / 1,000 = <strong>339.29 Liters</strong></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Example 2 -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 shadow-2xs dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white text-sm">
            Example 2: Diameter &amp; Height Mode
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-1">
            <strong>Problem:</strong> A standard beverage can has a diameter of 6.6 cm and a height of 12.2 cm. Calculate its internal capacity.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-2 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-3 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-950/60 space-y-1">
            <p>1. Radius = d / 2 = 6.6 / 2 = 3.3 cm</p>
            <p>2. Volume = π × (3.3)² × 12.2 = π × 10.89 × 12.2 ≈ 417.4 cm³ (or 417.4 mL)</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Example 3 -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 shadow-2xs dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white text-sm">
            Example 3: Hollow Cylindrical Pipe Material Volume
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-1">
            <strong>Problem:</strong> A cast-iron drainage pipe has an outer diameter D = 16 cm, an inner diameter d = 14 cm, and length h = 200 cm. Find the volume of cast iron.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-2 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-3 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-950/60 space-y-1">
            <p>1. Outer Radius R = 8 cm, Inner Radius r = 7 cm</p>
            <p>2. Ring Cross-Section = π × (R² − r²) = π × (64 − 49) = 15π ≈ 47.12 cm²</p>
            <p>3. Shell Volume = 47.12 × 200 = <strong>9,424.78 cm³</strong></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Example 4 -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 shadow-2xs dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white text-sm">
            Example 4: Reverse Calculation (Find Height)
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-1">
            <strong>Problem:</strong> You need a cylindrical container to hold exactly 2,000 cm³ of liquid, with a base radius of 7 cm. How tall must it be?
          </p>
          <div class="mt-2 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-3 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-950/60 space-y-1">
            <p>1. Base Area = π × 7² = 49π ≈ 153.938 cm²</p>
            <p>2. Height h = V / Base Area = 2,000 / 153.938 ≈ <strong>12.99 cm</strong></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Example 5 -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 shadow-2xs dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white text-sm">
            Example 5: Oblique Cylinder with Slant Angle
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-1">
            <strong>Problem:</strong> A slanted architectural column has a base radius of 20 cm, a slanted side length L = 150 cm, and leans at an angle θ = 60° to the horizontal foundation. Find its volume.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-2 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-3 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-950/60 space-y-1">
            <p>1. Perpendicular Height h = L × sin(60°) = 150 × 0.86603 ≈ 129.90 cm</p>
            <p>2. Base Area = π × (20)² = 400π ≈ 1,256.64 cm²</p>
            <p>3. Volume = 1,256.64 × 129.90 ≈ <strong>163,237 cm³ (163.24 Liters)</strong></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Real-World Applications</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Cylinders are everywhere in nature and human engineering because the circle provides the maximum cross-sectional area for any given perimeter, minimizing material weight while resisting internal pressure evenly:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc list-inside">
          <li><strong>Storage Tanks &amp; Silos:</strong> Vertical grain silos, crude oil storage tanks, and municipal water towers rely on cylindrical geometry to distribute hoop stress evenly without weak corner joints.</li>
          <li><strong>Pipelines &amp; Plumbing:</strong> Water pipes, sewer mains, and natural gas lines use hollow cylindrical shells to optimize volumetric flow rates while keeping pipe walls resilient against hydraulic pressure.</li>
          <li><strong>Hydraulic Cylinders:</strong> Heavy machinery (excavators, dump trucks, aircraft landing gear) uses hydraulic fluid displacement across a cylindrical bore to exert immense linear force.</li>
          <li><strong>Beverage Cans &amp; Food Tins:</strong> Aluminum cans are manufactured as lightweight right circular cylinders with domed bottoms for optimal stacking, refrigeration, and carbonation retention.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Engine Cylinder Displacement (Swept Volume)</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          In automotive and mechanical engineering, the displacement of an internal combustion engine is calculated as the sum of the swept volumes of all its cylinders. For a single cylinder:
        </p>
        <div class="my-2 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/80 p-3 font-mono text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          Swept Volume (V_d) = (π × B² × S) / 4
        </div>
        <p>
          Where <strong>B</strong> is the cylinder bore diameter and <strong>S</strong> is the piston stroke length. For an engine with <em>N</em> cylinders, the total engine displacement is <code>Total Displacement = V_d × N</code> (commonly measured in cubic centimeters, cc, or liters).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Surface Area to Volume Ratio (SA/V)</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The surface area of a closed right circular cylinder includes its lateral wall and its two circular ends:
        </p>
        <div class="my-2 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/80 p-3 font-mono text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          Total Surface Area (A) = 2πrh + 2πr²
        </div>
        <p>
          The ratio of surface area to volume (<code>SA/V = (2πrh + 2πr²) / (πr²h) = (2/r) + (2/h)</code>) governs how rapidly a cylinder loses or absorbs heat. A tall, thin cylinder has a much higher SA/V ratio and cools down rapidly, while a squat cylinder (where height approximately equals diameter) minimizes heat transfer and material costs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">10 Common Mistakes When Calculating Cylinder Volume</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <ol class="space-y-2 list-decimal list-inside">
          <li><strong>Using Diameter Instead of Radius:</strong> Forgetting to divide the diameter by 2 before squaring leads to a volume that is <strong>4 times too large</strong> (since <code>d² = 4r²</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Forgetting to Square the Radius:</strong> Writing <code>π × r × h</code> instead of <code>π × r² × h</code> produces a surface measurement rather than a volumetric capacity.</li>
          <li><strong>Using Slant Length as Height:</strong> In an oblique cylinder, the slanted edge length <em>L</em> is always longer than the perpendicular height <em>h</em>. You must use <code>h = L × sin(θ)</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Inner Radius Greater Than Outer Radius:</strong> In a hollow pipe calculation, the outer dimension must always be strictly larger than the inner dimension (<code>R &gt; r</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Mixing Dimensional Units:</strong> Combining radius in centimeters with height in meters without converting first yields nonsensical values. Always harmonize units before multiplying.</li>
          <li><strong>Confusing Liquid Ounces with Weight Ounces:</strong> Fluid ounces measure volumetric capacity, whereas ounces measure mass. Always use density to convert.</li>
          <li><strong>Confusing Surface Area with Volume:</strong> Surface area measures the outer 2D skin in square units; volume measures the interior 3D capacity in cubic units.</li>
          <li><strong>Assuming Ellipses Use Simple Radius:</strong> For an elliptical cylinder, the base area is <code>π × a × b</code> where <em>a</em> and <em>b</em> are semi-major and semi-minor axes, not standard radii.</li>
          <li><strong>Rounding Pi Prematurely:</strong> Truncating π to 3.14 for large industrial tanks creates significant engineering discrepancy. Always use high-precision <code>Math.PI</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Forgetting Pipe Wall Thickness Factor of Two:</strong> When converting outer diameter <em>D</em> to inner diameter <em>d</em>, remember <code>d = D − 2t</code> because the wall exists on both sides of the diameter.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Related Mathematical &amp; Geometry Calculators</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>Explore other precision calculators across our math and engineering suite:</p>
