  <Fragment slot="faq">
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
          What is the formula for the volume of a cylinder?
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          The formula is <code>V = π × r² × h</code>, where <em>V</em> represents volume, <em>r</em> is the radius of the circular base, and <em>h</em> is the perpendicular height. It signifies multiplying the 2D area of the circular base (<code>πr²</code>) by its perpendicular extrusion height.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
          How do I calculate cylinder volume from diameter?
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Because diameter <em>d</em> is twice the radius (<code>d = 2r</code>), radius is <code>r = d / 2</code>. Substituting this into the volume equation gives <code>V = (π × d² × h) / 4</code>. Square the diameter, multiply by π and height, then divide by 4.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
          How do I find the height when volume and radius are known?
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Rearrange the volume formula to solve for height: <code>h = V / (π × r²)</code>. Calculate the circular base area (<code>πr²</code>), then divide the total volume by that area.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
          How do I find the radius from volume and height?
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Rearrange the formula to isolate radius: <code>r = √(V / (π × h))</code>. Divide the volume by <code>(π × h)</code> and compute the square root. The diameter is then <code>d = 2r</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
          How is the volume of a hollow cylinder or pipe calculated?
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Subtract the inner core volume from the outer cylinder volume: <code>V = π × (R² − r²) × h</code>, where <em>R</em> is the outer radius, <em>r</em> is the inner radius, and <em>h</em> is the length. Using diameters, <code>V = [π × (D² − d²) × h] / 4</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
          Does an oblique (slanted) cylinder use a different volume formula?
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          No. By Cavalieri's Principle, any oblique cylinder with circular bases has the exact same volume formula <code>V = π × r² × h</code>, provided <em>h</em> is the strictly perpendicular distance between the two parallel base planes, not the slanted side length.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
          What is the difference between perpendicular height and slant length?
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Perpendicular height (<em>h</em>) is the orthogonal, shortest vertical distance measured at a 90° angle between the parallel bases. Slant length (<em>L</em>) is the length along the tilted lateral side. They are related by <code>h = L × sin(θ)</code>, where <em>θ</em> is the slant angle relative to the base plane.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
          Why is cylinder volume expressed in cubic units?
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Volume measures three-dimensional physical space. When you multiply a 2D circular area (units squared, e.g. cm²) by a linear height (units, e.g. cm), the resulting dimensional product is <code>cm² × cm = cm³</code> (cubic centimeters).
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
          How many liters are in one cubic meter?
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          There are exactly <strong>1,000 liters</strong> in one cubic meter (1 m³ = 1,000 L). Similarly, 1 liter equals 1,000 cubic centimeters (1 L = 1,000 cm³ = 1,000 mL).
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
          How is cylinder volume converted to weight or mass?
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Mass equals density multiplied by volume: <code>Mass = Density × Volume</code>. For freshwater at 4°C, the density is approximately 1,000 kg/m³ (or 1 gram per cm³). Therefore, 1 liter of pure water weighs almost exactly 1 kilogram (2.205 pounds).
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
